## Intuition
Every generated token looks back at the keys and values of all earlier tokens. The KV cache keeps them so they are computed once. It trades memory for compute, and the memory grows with the length of the context and with the number of sequences served at the same time.

## The math
The size of the cache for one sequence:

$$
M_{KV} = 2 \cdot L \cdot H_{kv} \cdot d_{head} \cdot b \cdot T
$$

- $L$ is the number of layers, and $H_{kv}$ the number of key/value heads (fewer than query heads with grouped-query attention).
- $d_{head}$ is the size of one head, $b$ the bytes per value (2 for fp16), and $T$ the tokens in the context.
- The leading 2 is for keys and values.

At batch size 1 a decoded token reads all the weights $W$ and the cache once, so memory bandwidth $B$ sets a ceiling:

$$
\text{tokens per second} \lesssim \frac{B}{W + M_{KV}}
$$

## Numbers to remember
Table: An 8B model in fp16, on an M4 Max
| Quantity | Value | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cache per token | 128 KiB | 2 · 32 · 8 · 128 · 2 bytes |
| Cache at 8k tokens | 1 GiB | per sequence |
| Weights | 16 GB | 8 billion parameters · 2 bytes |
| Memory bandwidth | 546 GB/s | the chip's rating |
| Decode ceiling | about 34 tokens per second | 546 GB/s ÷ 16 GB |

## Hardware implications
- Decode is limited by memory bandwidth, not by compute: each token reads every weight.
- Batching shares the weight reads between sequences, but not the cache, because every sequence has its own.
- Weights in 4 bits are about 4 GB, so the ceiling rises about four times, until the cache dominates.

## Pitfalls
> [!warning] Count the cache, not only the weights
> A 16 GB model serving 32 sequences of 8k tokens needs another 32 GiB for the cache. That is where "it fit on my machine" goes wrong.
